\f0\b Request: 
\f1\b0 I am a process engineer, and I need to choose the pipe size for a new cooling water line so the plant manager can approve the purchase. I will use Visual Studio Code to open a Jupyter notebook and load a CSV catalog of candidate pipe sizes with their costs. For each size I will calculate the flow velocity, Reynolds number, friction factor, pressure drop, pump power and yearly total cost. I will screen out sizes that break the velocity limit and pick the lowest cost size that remains. Then I will use PowerPoint to build a one-slide cost versus pipe size chart for the approval meeting, and Microsoft Word to complete a one-page line sizing datasheet.\
\

\f0\b Task Description: 
\f1\b0 \
1. Read the design basis and the plant velocity limits in the line sizing datasheet in Microsoft Word.\
2. Open the Jupyter notebook in Visual Studio Code, load the CSV pipe catalog, and calculate velocity, Reynolds number, friction factor, pressure drop, pump power and yearly total cost for each size. Screen out the sizes outside the velocity limits, pick the lowest cost size that remains, chart the costs, and cross-check the pressure drop with an independent friction method.\
3. Build a one-slide cost versus pipe size summary with the chart and the recommendation in PowerPoint.\
4. Complete the one-page line sizing datasheet with the selected size and the rejected sizes in Microsoft Word.\
5. Check the slide and datasheet numbers against the notebook results in Visual Studio Code.\
}
